  {
   "topic": "Sensing",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Collector current rises almost in proportion to irradiance: over 0.01 to 1 mW/cm² at VCE = 5 V the three sorts follow parallel straight lines on log-log axes. The part responds to both visible and near-infrared radiation."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bias and saturation",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Above roughly 0.5 V collector-emitter the light current is nearly flat with voltage and creeps up slowly toward 50 V; below that it falls steeply into saturation. At 1 mW/cm² and 950 nm the saturation voltage is at most 0.3 V with 0.1 mA flowing."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Light current grows with temperature, from about 0.77 of its 25 °C value at 0 °C to about 1.8 at 100 °C (VCE = 5 V, 1 mW/cm², 950 nm). Dark current at VCE = 20 V rises by roughly a decade every 20 °C, from about 1 nA at 25 °C to some 4 µA at 100 °C. Allowed dissipation is 100 mW up to 55 °C ambient and falls linearly to zero at 100 °C, for RthJA = 450 K/W."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Speed",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Switching times shorten as collector current rises: with VCE = 5 V and RL = 100 Ω, turn-off falls from about 5 µs at 1.3 mA to 2.1 µs at 10 mA and turn-on from about 4 µs to 1.5 µs. Collector-emitter capacitance falls from about 5.6 pF near 0.1 V to 1.3 pF at 50 V."
  },
